  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 PM EST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\n- Rain is still expected Sunday\n\nThe forecast has remained very stable with regard to the cold\nfront on Sunday. Rain will spread into Western Lower Michigan\nduring the morning on Sunday and sweep through during the day into\nthe evening hours. Precipitation is still expected to be on the\nlight side, generally a quarter inch or less. The moisture is \ndeep along and ahead of the front, but it will just not hold for \nvery long as it sweeps through. The deepest moisture and lift will\nonly be in place for 6 hours or so which will keep totals down. \n\nTonight we will be mostly clear much of the time with clouds\nmoving in towards daybreak. Behind the front some clearing will\ntry to take place Sunday night, but with cold air advection lake\nstratocumulus production will commence.\n\n- Light lake effect snow is possible Monday/Monday evening\n\nThere will be a period of time from Monday into Monday night where\nlake effect parameters try to come together. The two main issues\nwill be a lack of deep moisture and winds that will eventually\nback to the northeast/off shore into Tuesday. Moisture in the\n1000-700mb layer is often looked at for the health and viability\nof steadier/heavier snow. We do not have significant RH in this\nlayer. The 70 pct RH threshold is somewhat of a minimum that is\nnecessary for more significant snow. We occasionally touch the \n70-80 pct level across Central Lower Michigan (near and north of \nM-46). As is almost always the case, there is better moisture in \nthe 1000-850mb layer given the more shallow nature of this lake \neffect snow event. Bottom line given some moisture in the low \nlevels, delta T's near 20C and a period of cyclonic upper flow we \nare looking at flurries and light snow showers especially Monday \nafternoon and Monday evening across Central and Western Lower \nMichigan. Most areas will have little to no accumulation. However,\nits not out of the question near the U.S. 10 corridor that we see\na light dusting (trace amounts to possible a half inch). \n\n- Holiday weather is trending towards more precipitation\n\nThe deterministic models have been trending towards more\nprecipitation out towards Thanksgiving and into Friday of next\nweek. The upper low is trending towards the Great Lakes with\nsuccessive model runs. While the surface low goes up the eastern\nseaboard, there is inverted troughing that extends back into the \nGreat Lakes. The ensembles of the GFS and ECMWF have been \nsteadfast that there will be precipitation Thursday into Friday \nand now the deterministic runs are coming around to that theme. \nPrecipitation chances are centered on Thanksgiving afternoon and \nevening into Friday night. Precipitation may end up being a mix of\nrain and snow with temperatures being marginal especially at the \nsurface. Some accumulating snow is a possibility, especially \nThursday night up across the higher ground of Central Lower \nMichigan. Details will come more in focus as we move into the \nearly portion of the work week.",
